From: hbbtv2 <hbbtv2@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] WPE SHM: fast frame read-back through an ION pixmap surface (Dreambox)

glReadPixels() from a GPU render target runs at ~14 MB/s on the Dreambox vendor EGL (VideoCore V), which makes every large repaint cost
0.4-0.8 s at 1920x1080. That EGL can render into "pixmap" surfaces on memory the caller provides (struct dmegl_pixmap_handle: physical
address + CPU mapping). Allocate such memory from the ION BMEM heap, blit the bounding box of the damaged rectangles of the frame's
framebuffer into a pixmap surface with the GPU (glBlitFramebuffer, upside down, ~10-20 ms), wait, and copy the rectangles out of the
mapping with a NEON loop (vldmia: the mapping is uncached, glibc memcpy would run at 27 MB/s) that also swaps red and blue (the
framebuffer is RGBA, the shared bitmap BGRA).

Measured on a DM900, ZDF media library, 1920x1080: full frame 850 ms -> 90 ms, typical partial frame 190-240 ms -> 50 ms; the exact comparison
with Skia's readPixels (WEBKIT_SHM_READBACK_PIXMAP=verify) reports 0 differing pixels.

WEBKIT_SHM_READBACK_PIXMAP: unset or 1 = on (default), 0 = off, verify = on + compare every rect with Skia. Any failure (no /dev/ion, no pixmap
surface, GL error) switches the feature off for the process and Skia's readPixels() is used, as before.
